# =============================================================================
# DESIGN building block — widgets, screen shapes, and the control tree
# =============================================================================
# What `grammapy` used to hold as Python combinator objects, authored as facts in
# the same shape as the other four blocks. The CHECKS over these facts are systems
# (`design.py`) because each one needs something a `head when body` rule cannot
# say — a universal ("provides EVERY required capability"), a negation ("no
# handler covers this signal"), or a pairwise comparison ("two widgets, and not
# the same one"). Everything that IS expressible as a rule is a rule, here.
#
# ⭐ Swapping `textual.cnl` for a `web.cnl` means editing the `writes` lines here
# too — the widget vocabulary is the toolkit's, and this block is where the design
# layer meets it.

#-- Each widget and the screen slot it WRITES ---------------------------------
# The frame rule: a set of widgets composes iff their writes are pairwise
# disjoint. Two widgets claiming one slot is interference, caught at design time
# rather than as a UI that overwrites itself.
input_value    writes screen.amount
button_widget  writes screen.checkout
result_label   writes screen.result
styled_label   writes screen.result.style
modal_confirm  writes screen.gate

#-- The widgets every checkout screen carries, whatever the rules derive ------
input_value   always_placed yes
button_widget always_placed yes
result_label  always_placed yes

?w placed_for ?cart when ?w always_placed yes and ?cart has_checkout yes

#-- ...and the widget a bridge-ADMITTED feature brings with it ----------------
# This is the seam back to the bridge: a UX feature is realized by a capability,
# and an admitted capability is a widget on the screen. Delete `styled_label
# supported_by textual` from the library block and this places nothing.
?cap placed_for ?cart when ?feat admitted_for ?cart and ?feat realized_by ?cap

#-- The screen shapes on offer, and what each PROVIDES ------------------------
one_screen     produces screen
confirm_screen produces screen
one_screen     is_default screen
confirm_screen provides confirmation

#-- What a UX feature DEMANDS of the screen shape -----------------------------
# `confirmation_step` is not satisfied by any old screen: it needs one that
# provides a confirmation. That is what FORCES the shape to flip.
confirmation_step demands confirmation

#-- The control tree: who emits the signal, and who handles it ----------------
# The completion leaf emits `needs_confirmation` exactly when the action is
# irreversible; the confirm screen is the only thing that handles it. A design in
# which the signal is emitted and nothing above it handles it is REJECTED.
complete_purchase emits_if action_irreversible
confirm_screen    handles  needs_confirmation

?n emits ?sig when ?n emits_if action_irreversible and ?cart action_irreversible yes and needs_confirmation is_signal ?sig
needs_confirmation is_signal needs_confirmation

# A node sits INSIDE a handler only when that handler is the chosen screen.
?n inside ?s when ?n emits ?sig and ?s handles ?sig and ?s chosen_for ?cart
